      - 1915
      - 1916
      - 1917
      - 1918
      - 1919
      - 1920
      - 1921
      - 1922
    series:
      - {name: Output, values: [100, 101, 112, 122, 124, 122, 143, 152, 151, 126, 155, 159, 153, 177, 184, 169, 189, 225, 227, 223, 218, 231, 179, 240], color: '#3d3833'}
      - {name: Capital, values: [100, 107, 114, 122, 131, 138, 149, 163, 176, 185, 198, 208, 216, 226, 236, 244, 266, 298, 335, 366, 387, 407, 417, 431], color: '#2f6b8f'}
      - {name: Labour, values: [100, 105, 110, 118, 123, 116, 125, 133, 138, 121, 140, 144, 145, 152, 154, 149, 154, 182, 196, 200, 193, 193, 147, 161], color: '#b5482e'}
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 460
    y_label: Index, 1899 = 100
    x_label: US manufacturing
    label_size: 22
sources:
    - id: cd1928
      title: A Theory of Production
      authors: Charles W. Cobb and Paul H. Douglas
      year: 1928
      finding: 'US manufacturing 1899–1922 (1899 = 100): fixed capital rose to 431, wage earners to 161, physical production (Day index) to 240 by 1922; fitted P = 1.01·L^(3/4)·C^(1/4).'
    - id: hd1937
      title: The Theory of Marginal Productivity Tested by Data for Manufacturing in Victoria
      authors: Marjorie L. Handsaker and Paul H. Douglas
      year: 1937
      finding: 'Recalls the 1928 study: with exponents summing to one, marginal productivity theory predicts labour receives 75% and capital 25% of the value product, ''found to be approximately the case''.'
    - id: sw2019
      title: The Cobb-Douglas production function revisited
      authors: Roman G. Smirnov and Kunpeng Wang
      year: 2019
      finding: Tabulates the Cobb–Douglas 1928 index series (log form) for 1899–1922.
      url: https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.06739

    text v431 "431" #2f6b8f at.data [1921.2,452] cites ["cd1928","sw2019"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text v161 "161" #b5482e at.data [1922.6,128] cites ["cd1928"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text v240 "240" #3d3833 at.data [1921.4,292] cites ["cd1928"] font_size 40 hidden true
    scratch cdcard cites ["cd1928","hd1937"] work ["Cobb & Douglas, 1928",{"tex":"Y = 1.01\\,L^{3/4}\\,K^{1/4}"},"wages ≈ 3/4 of output"] x 390 y 515 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "How does a firm turn inputs into output?"

How does a firm turn inputs into output? Start with all of American manufacturing, from 1899 to 1922.
    draw axes

B: Over those years the factories' machines and buildings, their capital, grew more  (@draw v431@)than fourfold.
    draw line_2

The number of workers grew too, but only by about s (@draw v161@)ixty percent.
    draw line_3

    ask "Draw the output line from 1899 to 1922." on line_1 from 1899 store g voice b
        intro: Four times the machines, one and a half times the people. Draw where you think output went.
        right: Two hundred and forty: you put it between the two, close.
        wrong: Here is the real line beside yours: it ended at 240, more than double, far below the machines.

Output ended at two h (@draw v240@)undred and forty: more than doubled, well above the workers, far below the machines.

B: So output landed much closer to the workers than to the machines. Capital alone can't be the story.
    highlight line_1

A mathematician, Charles Cobb, and an economist, Paul Douglas, turned that into a formula in 1928.
    draw cdcard_box cdcard_line_1

Output Y equals labour L to the power three quarters, times capital K to the power one quarter.
    draw cdcard_line_2

B: A recipe like that is a production function: how much comes out for each mix of what goes in.
    highlight cdcard_line_2 effect underline

And the odd part: labour (@highlight cdcard_line_2 part L^{3/4}@)'s weight, three quarters, was close to the share of manufa (@draw cdcard_line_3@)cturing's income that went to wages.

B: Why would a number in a recipe match a pay packet? To see it,  (@erase v431 v161@)shrink the economy down to one small firm.
    highlight cdcard_line_3

    pause 0.3

## One more pair of hands
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation:
      - Q(L) = A*K^0.25*L^0.75
      - Q(L) = 25*L
    variable: L
    params:
      A: {value: 29.73, fixed: true}
      K: {value: 2, min: 1, max: 8, fixed: true, label: kilns K}
      'n': {value: 4, min: 1, max: 10, step: 1, label: workers}
    editable:
      - 'n'
    drag: false
    show_equation: false
    x_range:
      - 0
      - 10
    y_range:
      - 0
      - 250
    x_label: Workers, L
    y_label: Kilos a day, Q
    marks:
      - {kind: point, at: 'n', guides: false}
      - {kind: tangent, at: 'n'}

    icon shop size 70 set twemoji x 275 y 590 icon_look picture of fish hidden true
    text kilns "{eq.K:0} kilns" #2f6b8f x 390 y 590 font_size 38 hidden true
    text ry "{eq.point_y:0} kilos" x 800 y 290 font_size 40 hidden true
    text rmp "+{eq.tangent_slope:0} kilos\nfrom one more worker" #b5482e x 790 y 220 font_size 34 hidden true
    text dim "diminishing returns" #b5482e x 790 y 150 font_size 36 hidden true
    text dbl "double?" #8f887c at.data [7.3,215] font_size 30 hidden true
    text share "wages ≈ 3/4 of Q" #3d3833 x 390 y 530 font_size 38 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch mcard work [{"tex":"MP_L = \\tfrac{3}{4}\\,Q/L"},{"tex":"w = MP_L"},{"tex":"w\\,L = \\tfrac{3}{4}\\,Q"}] x 400 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "One more pair of hands"

B: A formula for a whole country's factories is hard to picture. So take one firm: Ha (@draw shop@)rbourtown's new smokehouse.
    draw axes

B: Illustrative numbers. It (@draw kilns@) has two kilns, its capital K, and hires workers to gut, salt and hang the f (@highlight x_label@)ish: its labour L.

With four workers it smokes a hund (@draw point ry@)red kilos of fish a day. A firm's output we ca (@highlight y_label@)ll Q; Y stays for a whole economy.

    draw curve_1 dbl

    quiz "Eight workers, the same two kilns: how many kilos a day?" judge false voice b
        intro: Now double the crew to eight, and keep the same two kilns. How many kilos?
        right: Here comes the curve.
        * About 200: double
        * About 170
        * About 120

(@draw curve_0@) About a hundred a (@animate params.n 8 duration 1.5@)nd seventy, not two hundred. The new hands queue for the same kilns.

B: The extra kilos one more worker  (@draw tangent rmp@)adds is called the marginal product of labour. On the curve, it's the slope.

At four workers, one more adds about nineteen kilos. At eight, about sixteen.
    blocking false

    animate params.n 4 duration 1.2

    pause 0.6

    animate params.n 8 duration 1.2

    erase tangent

Hold one input fixed, keep adding another, and each addition adds less. Th (@draw dim@)at's diminishing returns.

    erase curve_1 dbl

B: Try it: drag the point to add workers one by one, and watch the extra kilos shrink.
    explore

    erase dim

    animate params.n 8 duration 0.6

Now give the smokehouse two more kilns, and keep the crew.
    highlight kilns

B: The whole curve lifts, and it gets steeper: with more kilns, every worker adds more. The inputs work together.
    animate params.K 4 duration 2.5 ghost true

And the pay packet: hire until the last wor (@highlight rmp@)ker's extra kilos just cover her wage, and with this recipe wages come  (@draw share@)to three quarters of output.

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want to see why it comes out at three quarters?

@math

    fade axes curve_0 point ry rmp kilns shop to 0.25

    erase mathq share

With this recipe, the slope works out to three quarters of output per worker.
    draw mcard_box mcard_line_1

B: A firm that takes the wage as given hires until the last worker's marginal product equals the wage.
    draw mcard_line_2

Multiply by the number of workers: the wage bill is three quarters of output, whatever L and K are.
    draw mcard_line_3

B: And capital's owners get the other quarter.
    erase mcard

@test

    fade axes curve_0 point ry rmp kilns shop to 1

    erase mathq

    pause 0.3

## Same fish, different mix
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation:
      - K(L) = (100/(A*L^0.75))^4
      - K(L) = (200/(A*L^0.75))^4
    variable: L
    params:
      A: {value: 29.73, fixed: true}
    editable: []
    drag: false
    show_equation: false
    x_range:
      - 0
      - 10
    y_range:
      - 0
      - 18
    x_label: Workers, L
    y_label: Kilns, K
    marks:
      - {kind: point, at: 4, id: p42, label: '4 workers, 2 kilns'}
      - {kind: point, at: 2, id: p216, label: '2 workers, 16 kilns'}
      - {kind: point, at: 8, curve: 1, id: p84, label: '8 workers, 4 kilns'}
sources:
    - id: robinson1953
      title: The Production Function and the Theory of Capital
      authors: Joan Robinson
      year: 1953
      finding: Review of Economic Studies 21(2). Capital goods cannot be added up without valuing them, and their value depends on the rate of profit, which the aggregate production function was meant to determine.
    - id: samuelson1966
      title: A Summing Up
      authors: Paul A. Samuelson
      year: 1966
      finding: Quarterly Journal of Economics 80(4). Concedes that reswitching is possible, so the simple neoclassical parable of capital 'cannot be universally valid'.
    - id: hd1937
      title: The Theory of Marginal Productivity Tested by Data for Manufacturing in Victoria
      authors: Marjorie L. Handsaker and Paul H. Douglas
      year: 1937
      finding: The 1928 Cobb–Douglas study used exponents 3/4 and 1/4 made to sum to one; whether the sum should be forced to one is raised as an open question.

    text q100 "100 kilos" #2f6b8f at.data [1.1,10.5] font_size 34 hidden true
    text q200 "200 kilos" #b5482e at.data [6.1,17] font_size 34 hidden true
    text crs "double both → double output" at.data [8,14] font_size 34 hidden true
    text sum1 "3/4 + 1/4 = 1" at.data [8,12.3] cites ["hd1937"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text kq "K = kilns + boats\n+ computers… in $?" #b5482e at.data [8.5,9.6] cites ["robinson1953"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text sam "Samuelson, 1966:\nnot in general" #3d3833 at.data [8.7,6.9] cites ["samuelson1966"] font_size 30 hidden true
    text qmark "?" #b5482e at.data [0.8,16.3] font_size 72 hidden true
    portrait robinson x 880 y 540 width 150 of "Joan Robinson" cameo true hidden true
    text st1 "diminishing returns:\nconsensus" #2f6b8f at.data [8.2,14.5] font_size 36 hidden true
    text st2 "capital as one number:\ncontested" #b5482e at.data [8.2,11.3] cites ["samuelson1966"] font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Same fish, different mix"

Kilns and hands work together. But can one stand in for the other?
    draw axes

B: This curve is (@draw curve_0 q100@) every mix of workers and kilns that smokes the same hundred kilos a day. It's called an isoquant: same quantity.

Four worke (@draw p42@)rs and two kilns is one point on it. With only two wor (@draw p216@)kers, you would need sixteen kilns.

B: So swapping hands for kilns gets harder the further you push it. That's why the curve bends.
    highlight curve_0

Now doubl (@draw curve_1 q200 p84@)e both: eight workers, four kilns. Output doubles too, to two hundred kilos.

B: That's constan (@draw crs@)t returns to scale. Cobb and Douglas didn't test it in 1928: they chose weights that ad (@draw sum1@)d up to one, three quarters plus a quarter.

But what is K for a  (@draw qmark@)whole economy?
    erase crs sum1 p216

(@draw robinson@) Joan Robinson asked exactly that in 1953: kilns, boats and  (@draw kq@)computers can only be added up in money,

and their money value depends on the rate of profit, the very thing the recipe was meant to explain.
    highlight kq

B: Paul Samuelson and Robert Solow defended the recipe from MIT. In 1966 Samuelson conceded t (@draw sam@)hat the simple story of capital can't hold in general. Most economist (@erase robinson@)s still use it as a handy approximation.

    erase kq sam

How sure are we? That returns di (@draw st1@)minish when one input is held fixed: consensus.

That a whole (@draw st2@) economy's capital fits in one number: contested.

    erase st1 st2 qmark

    hide axes x_label y_label curve_0 curve_1 q100 q200 p42 p42_guides p42_label p84 p84_guides p84_label x_ticks y_ticks

    cards sortc bins ["One input added","All inputs scaled"] check each
        * "More cooks, same kitchen" bin "One input added"
        * "A second, identical factory" bin "All inputs scaled"
        * "More fertiliser, same field" bin "One input added"
        All inputs scaled: Twice the trucks and drivers

    ask "One input added, or all scaled? Sort each card." intro "Two new cases to finish." on sortc store f stage own voice b
        right: All four: diminishing returns need something held fixed; returns to scale scale everything.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. Does anything stay fixed? Here, cooks and fertiliser add one input; the factory and trucks scale all.

    erase sortc

    show axes x_label y_label curve_0 curve_1 q100 q200 p42 p42_guides p42_label p84 p84_guides p84_label x_ticks y_ticks

    quiz "A bakery doubles its bakers and its ovens, and its output rises 2.5 times. What is that?"
        right: Increasing returns to scale: everything doubled, and output more than doubled.
        wrong: Nothing was held fixed, and output more than doubled: here, increasing returns to scale.
        * Diminishing returns
        * Constant returns to scale
        + Increasing returns to scale

So a firm turns inputs into output by a recipe, and while something stays fixed, every extra hand adds a little less.
    highlight curve_0 curve_1

B: What that does to the cost of each extra kilo is the next question.
    highlight p84

    pause 0.3

